export const body = "<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p>Dismemberment is not a pleasant word to say, and it's certainly not an easy feature to implement. In video games, dismemberment refers to the visual, and sometimes mechanical, depiction of bodily damage.</p>\n<p>For example, <em>Monster Hunter World</em> allows players to cut the tails off certain monsters to weaken them. Meanwhile, in <em>God of War (2018)</em>, dismemberment only occurs during preset executions to improve the feel of combat. So, we will categorize them respectively as \"strategic dismemberment\" and \"non-strategic dismemberment.\"</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/ab87c35b742991e40142.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/ab87c35b742991e40142.webp\" width=\"480\" height=\"270\" style=\"--source-width:272px\" alt=\"Illustration 1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/3cd0a6b668134c7277db.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/3cd0a6b668134c7277db.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"646\" style=\"--source-width:272px\" alt=\"Illustration 2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/056b31ff28afee312ba7.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/056b31ff28afee312ba7.webp\" width=\"807\" height=\"455\" style=\"--source-width:289px\" alt=\"Illustration 3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/935e90812cfc941702eb.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/935e90812cfc941702eb.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"767\" style=\"--source-width:289px\" alt=\"Illustration 4\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>Non-strategic dismemberment simply serves a cosmetic function, informing the player of his impact on the enemy. Another example similar to <em>God of War (2018</em><em>)</em> is <em>Ninja Gaiden 2</em>. In this game, your blade can shed blood and remove body parts with any attack.</p>\n<p>In <em>Doom Eternal</em>, every bullet shot will disintegrate the demons piece by piece, exposing their flesh over time according to the amount of damage they receive. This feature (called \"Destructible demon\") delivers immediate visual feedback to the player while simultaneously portraying the brutal fantasy intended by the developers.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>With strategic dismemberment, severed limbs also entail gameplay implications, such as disabling a certain ability of the enemy or granting you, the player, some kind of bonus.</p>\n<p>In <em>The Last of Us Part II</em>, players can vaporize an enemy's legs to incapacitate it, forcing it to crawl instead of barreling toward them.</p>\n<p><em>The Surge</em> <em>2</em> has a targeting system which gives players the control over which body part to dismember. This system directly ties to loot, which means enemies will drop items corresponding to their removed body part. For that reason, the most effective tactic to acquire a new weapon is to target the wielder's right arm.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/a5fb229f915472b0e564.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/a5fb229f915472b0e564.webp\" width=\"670\" height=\"376\" style=\"--source-width:272px\" alt=\"Illustration 5\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/ba1de4ebee020ddf3be5.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/ba1de4ebee020ddf3be5.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"648\" style=\"--source-width:272px\" alt=\"Illustration 6\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/cbeb93aa757a8f262240.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/cbeb93aa757a8f262240.webp\" width=\"670\" height=\"376\" style=\"--source-width:289px\" alt=\"Illustration 7\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/2e77cf02df3b0614a0c1.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/2e77cf02df3b0614a0c1.webp\" width=\"670\" height=\"376\" style=\"--source-width:289px\" alt=\"Illustration 8\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>Real-time dismemberment, of course, takes a huge toll on the operating system. Therefore, developers occasionally have to . . . cut corners.</p>\n<p><em>Resident Evil 2 (2019)</em> has a sophisticated dismemberment system that registers every instant of bodily damage, rendering the gradual deterioration on the walking dead. This effect not only enhances the horror quality of the game, but also providing players with a tool to maneuver combat encounters. Sometimes, it's better to maim a zombie and run away to reserve your ammunition.</p>\n<p>In <em>Resident Evil 3 (2020)</em>, the developers decided to increase the enemy count per area to heighten the action aspect. Unfortunately, this decision ended up overtaxing the systems, leaving them no choice but to dial down the dismemberment tech from the last game. Bodily damage is still present, but it's nowhere near as detailed.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>To sum up, dismemberment can either be a mechanic or a visual feature. Other than the stress on operating systems, dismemberment also raises the age rating, which in turn restricts the potential audience for your game. So, be careful with it.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/19d29fcd71e986adb7c4.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/19d29fcd71e986adb7c4.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"646\" style=\"--source-width:272px\" alt=\"Illustration 9\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Or you can just make a LEGO game and get away with everything. </em><em></em></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div>";
